# COMPLETENESS SCORING

Judge how complete this document is as a production-ready ADR: every named component and data
contract should have every required field fully resolved — a confirmed lifecycle status, a real
ODCS `version`/`status`/`schema.properties` — not a placeholder standing in for what nobody has
confirmed. `version: not-specified`, `status: not-specified`, an empty `schema.properties: {}`
next to an included contract: unresolved information dressed as a finished field, not a
genuinely empty section.

**A `new` contract's `version` and `status` are structural ODCS conventions, not transcript
facts — do not flag them as unconfirmed just because the transcript never said them out loud.**
A new contract's version is always `1.0.0`, and its status is `active` for any contract
currently in force (new, forward-updated, or unchanged) — nobody says "this is version 1.0.0" in
a meeting, the same way nobody says a brand-new contract's status is "active." Seeing exactly
those conventional values on a `new`/in-force contract is a correctly filled field, not evidence
of an unresolved gap. Only flag `version`/`status` when the VALUE itself is actually wrong: a version
other than `1.0.0` on a contract the document itself calls `new`; a `forward-update`/
`break-change` contract whose version was never bumped from a stated previous one; `active` on a
contract the transcript or clarifications say is being deprecated/retired; or a missing
`deprecated`/`retired` status on a contract the document itself says is being phased out. A
schema field with no declared `type`, or an array field with no `items` schema, is a real,
document-level gap — unlike version/status, nothing about a field's shape is a fixed convention,
so judge those purely by what the document actually shows.

`completeness_score` (0-100): 100 means every included item has every required field resolved,
no placeholder-like value anywhere. Deduct roughly in proportion to how much required structure
is placeholder/unresolved rather than confirmed fact — one contract with an unresolved
schema field out of an otherwise complete document should cost noticeably more than a few
points. Never deduct for a correctly-conventional `version`/`status` per the paragraph above.

`unresolved_points` — one entry per specific unresolved field/fact, e.g.
`"notification-created contract: items array has no declared item schema"`, `"Email Worker:
dependency list not confirmed"`. Empty only when `completeness_score` is 100.

A correct omission is not an unresolved point and doesn't reduce the score: a component/contract
the transcript only mentions in passing, with no confirmed role, is expected to be absent from
the affected-components/contracts sections. Only a field that IS present on an item the document
DOES include, but holds a placeholder value, counts.

Return JSON matching the requested schema, nothing else.

